    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "The Best Asphalt Sealer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-asphalt-sealer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should I seal my asphalt driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "As a general rule, a driveway should be sealed every 2 to 3 years. This allows the pavement to breathe while keeping up a strong protective barrier against the elements. Sealing too often (every single year) can cause the material to build up into a thick slab, resulting in severe surface tension that leads to hairline cracking and massive flaking.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can an asphalt sealer repair large cracks?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Sealers are designed solely to protect the surface and fill micro-fissures (up to ⅛ inch). Any gap larger than that requires a devoted asphalt crack filler or cold patch material to be applied and cured completely before the overarching sealing process begins.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use a squeegee or a brush?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both! The best method is pulling the heavy sealer down the driveway thinly using a wide, stiff rubber squeegee to force it into the pores of the asphalt, and then immediately back-brushing it to eliminate streaks and pull off excess material before it creates thick puddles that refuse to cure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why did my sealer flake off over the winter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Flaking is almost universally caused by one of three application errors: 1) Improper cleaning (the sealer stuck to dust instead of asphalt). 2) The sealer was applied too thick. 3) The sealer was applied over an area previously sealed with an incompatible product (e.g., applying water-based emulsion over an old coat of high VOC oil-based sealer without proper wear-down).",
          },
        },
