                </p>

                <h2 id="the-science-of-asphalt-degradation" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 font-sans">
                    The Science of Asphalt Degradation: Why Seal?
                </h2>
                
                <p>
                    Before reviewing products, it is vital to understand <em>why</em> sealers are necessary. Freshly laid asphalt is comprised of rock aggregates suspended in a heavy petroleum-based binder. Because it is a petroleum product, it is innately susceptible to two major chemical processes:
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Oxidation and UV Radiation:</strong> As the sun beats down on your driveway, ultraviolet rays execute a slow thermal degradation of the asphalt binder. The driveway turns from a rich, pliable black to an oxidized, brittle, and faded gray. As the binder becomes brittle, it loses flexibility, causing it to snap under the weight of vehicles rather than flex.</li>
                    <li><strong>Chemical Solvent Damage:</strong> "Like dissolves like" is a golden rule in chemistry. Because asphalt is petroleum-based, any oil, gasoline, or brake fluid dropped from your car instantly begins acting as a solvent, physically melting and dissolving the pavement matrix.</li>
                    <li><strong>The Freeze-Thaw Cycle:</strong> As the pavement becomes brittle and micro-fractures appear, water seeps into these cracks. If you live in a climate where temperatures drop below freezing, that trapped water freezes and expands by up to 9%, exerting massive hydraulic pressure that rips the asphalt apart from the inside out, creating potholes.</li>
                </ul>

                <p>
                    A high-quality topcoat sealer mitigates all three of these factors simultaneously. By applying a sacrificial layer of protective material, the sealer takes the brunt of the UV rays, repels water and prevents it from penetrating, and blocks automotive chemicals from melting the substrate.
                </p>

